.TH PKILL 1 "Riplix" "Ripple"
.SH NAME
pkill \- signal processes by name
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pkill
.RB [ \-SIGNAL ]
.RB [ \-x ]
.RB [ \-e ]
.RB [ \-\- ]
.I PATTERN
.SH DESCRIPTION
Signal all live processes whose names match the regular expression PATTERN.
The default signal is TERM. Without -x a matching substring is sufficient.
Process names are the first eight characters reported by ps, not full command
lines. PID 1, zombies, processes being forked, and pkill itself are excluded.
The kernel applies the normal signal permission checks.
.PP
Patterns support anchors, dot, character classes, grouping, alternation,
and *, + and ? repetition through the shared V8 regexp library. POSIX named
character classes and brace-counted repetition are not supported. Patterns
must be nonempty and at most 256 characters. Quote shell metacharacters.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-x
Require the entire process name to match.
.TP
.B \-e
Print each successfully signalled process name and PID. Normally success is silent.
.TP
.BI \- SIGNAL
Use a numeric or symbolic signal, with optional SIG prefix. Names are case
insensitive: -9, -KILL and -SIGKILL are equivalent. Signal 0 checks for a
matching process that the caller may signal, without sending a signal.
.TP
.BI \-\-signal " SIGNAL"
Select a signal. --signal=SIGNAL is also accepted.
.TP
.B \-l, \-\-list-signals
List supported signals.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Show usage.
.TP
.B \-\-
End option parsing, allowing a pattern beginning with a dash.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
pkill sleep
pkill -x lynx
pkill -HUP -x telnetd
pkill -9 -e '^(lynx|netsurf)$'
pkill -0 -x sleep; echo $?
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
0: at least one process was successfully signalled or checked.
1: no process could be signalled. 2: invalid arguments or regular expression.
3: process information could not be read.
.SH SEE ALSO
kill(1), ps(1)
